            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's definition of 'voice' as 'the words of the one who speaks' represents a standard and well-supported metonymy used throughout Scripture. The common interpretation broadly agrees, identifying 'voice' (especially God's voice) as authoritative communication, speech, or revelation. The book effectively demonstrates this through John 10:27, where hearing the Shepherd's voice means receiving and heeding His teaching or words. Because the book's core identification aligns tightly with the common consensus regarding covenantal and didactic passages, it is highly persuasive.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The symbol or figure of 'voice' represents the words, teaching, or message of the one speaking.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"The Shepherd's voice is His words.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter redefines 'love' almost exclusively as 'self-giving covenant allegiance embodied in the keeping [of commandments]', minimizing affection or relational warmth.\",\n    \"The chapter asserts that 'knowing' God is strictly 'the covenant relationship proved by the keeping' rather than also encompassing personal, relational acquaintance.\",\n    \"The chapter claims 'belief' is synonymous with submission and 'unbelief' with disobedience, collapsing the distinction between believing a truth and obeying a command.\",\n    \"The chapter merges faith and works into a single mechanism of 'trusting faithfulness' (obedience held steady), which could be challenged by Pauline distinctions between faith and works.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"When used symbolically, 'voice' (especially the voice of the Lord) often represents overwhelming divine power, presence, or majesty (such as thunder), not merely semantic 'words' or teaching.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"The acoustic or physical manifestation of divine authority, presence, and awe-inspiring power.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"SAME_CORE_BROADER\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"'Voice' is not restricted to spoken 'words' or didactic teaching, but frequently functions as an emblem of raw divine power or a personified cry where no specific words are articulated.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Psalm 29:3-9 describes the voice of the Lord as thundering over the waters, breaking cedars, and shaking the wilderness—emphasizing power rather than a semantic message.\",\n    \"Genesis 4:10 features the 'voice' of Abel's blood crying from the ground, personifying a demand for justice rather than communicating a specific set of words.\",\n    \"Revelation 1:15 compares Christ's voice to the sound of many waters, emphasizing overwhelming majesty.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Provides a precise, actionable explanation for covenantal texts (like John 10:5, 27) where 'hearing the voice' is functionally identical to heeding teaching and recognizing a speaker's doctrine.\",\n    \"Aligns with the frequent scriptural parallelism between hearing God's voice and obeying God's words/commandments.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Better accounts for poetic, apocalyptic, and theophanic texts where 'voice' highlights the awe, volume, or majesty of a sound rather than intelligible vocabulary.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"John 10:5\",\n    \"John 10:27\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
